SCHEMA-HASH-DECODE — PROBED 2026-10-16, BEFORE BUILDING. One finding changes the plan.

CHECK THESE WITH `--draft`: two of the three probes carry DELIBERATELY UNUSED warm-up
steps, whose only job is to bump the proof's fresh-name counter — the variable under
test. Strict mode correctly rejects an unused fact, so `--draft` is the right mode and
not a weakening here.

SCHEMA-HASH-DECODE-PLAN.md picks route A (re-derivation): "Do not store anything. On
demand, enumerate candidate instantiations, recompute `instanceHash` for each, and
report the ones that match." Its stated cost is "a scan over citation sites per query".

THAT UNDERSTATES THE COST, and the probes say by how much.

PROBE 1 — lambda-arg-is-counter-free.b4m. A schema instantiated at a LAMBDA argument
(`(fun k: Nat => add(k, ZERO) = k)`), twice, once after warm-up `fix` steps that bump
the proof's fresh-name counter. Result: ONE instance, one hash. The lambda's params
canonicalize BY POSITION in `hashTerm` (the `#` branch), so the counter does not reach
the hash. Good — and this is the case the plan's examples are all drawn from.

PROBE 2 — eigenvar-arg-depends-on-the-counter.b4m. A schema with a VALUE param,
instantiated INSIDE a `fix` at an argument that MENTIONS the fix's eigenvar, twice,
identically written, differing only in whether warm-up `fix` steps precede it:

    addZeroOnTheRight{adea7c551b3c7020}      <- no warm-up
    addZeroOnTheRight{e78561b481fdd421}      <- after one warm-up fix

TWO DISTINCT HASHES FOR THE SAME WRITTEN INSTANTIATION.

PROBE 3 — eigenvar-arg-without-the-warmup.b4m. The same file with the warm-up deleted:
both theorems now hash to `adea7c551b3c7020`. So the warm-up's extra `fix` was the only
difference, and the per-proof fresh counter is definitively the cause.

WHY. An eigenvar is `freshNamed`'d, so its name is `<prefix>#<counter>` where
`Prove.fresh_counter` counts every fresh name minted EARLIER IN THAT PROOF
(Prove.zig:284). `hashTerm`'s fvar case canonicalizes only lambda params (the `#`
branch, by position in `canon`); every OTHER fvar takes the `@` branch and hashes its
NAME BYTES (Schema.zig:120-127). An eigenvar is not a lambda param, so its counter
value is in the hash.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR ROUTE A. Re-deriving a hash from a citation site requires
reproducing the proof's fresh-counter state at that step — i.e. REPLAYING THE PROOF UP
TO THE INSTANTIATION, not elaborating the site in isolation. The plan's "walking proofs
for instantiation steps yields the candidate set directly" holds for the CANDIDATE SET
but not for the HASH: binding an argument needs a live `Elab` with scope, sort
resolution and the counter (Prove.bindSchemaArgs / bindLambdaArg, ~Prove.zig:2020-2105).

THREE WAYS FORWARD, and the choice is a real one:

  A'. RE-DERIVATION BY REPLAY. Run the prover again with a decode hook: at each
      instantiation, compute the hash and compare. Correct by construction, since it IS
      the same code path. Cost is one extra proving pass per query — acceptable on an
      error path, and the plan's own rationale ("slow is fine") covers it. No new data
      structure. This is the smallest honest version of route A.

  B. THE REVERSE SIDE TABLE the plan rejects. Its stated reason is the lock-discipline
      hazard around Context side tables ([[side-table-lock-discipline]]). That reason
      stands, but note the table would be written ONCE per instance at the naming site
      and read only by a query — not during proving — so it is not the shape that
      segfaulted. Worth re-weighing now that route A is known to need a replay.

  C. MAKE THE HASH COUNTER-FREE: canonicalize eigenvars the way lambda params already
      are. Then re-derivation works from the site alone, as the plan assumed. This is
      the most invasive but it fixes the underlying oddity — two identical
      instantiations currently mint TWO instances and prove the same thing twice, which
      is wasted work regardless of decoding. Note the hash's comment already records a
      PREVIOUS determinism bug of exactly this family (`cc800fe`, Indexes vs names), so
      this is the third iteration on the same question.

RECOMMENDATION: C, then A' becomes trivial. C is also the only one that makes the
duplicate-instance waste go away, and the hash's own doc comment says what it is for —
"it addresses a schema instance" — which two spellings of one instantiation violate.

NOT YET DISCUSSED WITH THE USER. The plan was approved on the understanding that
re-derivation is cheap and self-contained; probe 2 says it is not, so the choice above
should be theirs.
